  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 225 AM CST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nCurrently have a broad upper low situated over the Eastern Great \nLakes region with a couple different impulses approaching the West \nCoast. One is approaching Southern CA with a more robust upper wave \napproaching the Pacific Northwest. At the surface, cold front \nextends from the TX Panhandle into Southeast TX. \n\nSurface high pressure will remain in place today from the Upper \nMississippi Valley into the Southern Plains. This will provide \nplenty of sunshine and below normal temps, with highs around 50 \ndegrees. For tonight into Sat morning, the northern stream impulse \nwill move into the Pacific Northwest and Great Basin with the \nsouthern stream wave quickly tracking across Baja and into southwest \nTX. These systems will remain too far west for precip on Sat, but \nwill bring an increase in cloud cover. \n\nThere is good model agreement that the northern stream energy will \ndive southeast and close off into an upper low over the TX/OK \nPanhandles by Sun morning. This will start to spread rainfall \ninto the western portion of our forecast area Sat night and \nexpand southeast on Sunday. At this time it appears southern KS \nwill have the higher rainfall chances. The model trends with \nthis system have been anything but consistent and until it fully\ngets onshore, would expect more changes. Once this impulse \nmoves into the Arklatex region on Mon, we are looking for dry \nconditions to start the work week. A fast moving open wave is\nthen expected to lift out of the Central Great Basin and into \nthe Northern Plains by Wed afternoon. The main affect this \nimpulse will have will be some gusty south winds for Tue and \nespecially Wed.",
